## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep bit-slicing, 128-bit composition, and Morton (Z-order) interleaving in a branchless, portable way.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order interleaving
- Bit-slicing with masks and shifts (part1by1 technique)
- 64-bit to 128-bit composition without branches
- Endianness implications for MSB-first construction

## Code Example
```c
struct uint128_t {
    uint64_t hi, lo;
};

// Spread single bits to double-bit positions
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x ^ (x << 16)) & 0x0000ffff0000ffff;
    x = (x ^ (x << 8))  & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ff;
    x = (x ^ (x << 4))  & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0f;
    x = (x ^ (x << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333;
    x = (x ^ (x << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555;
    return x;
}

struct uint128_t interleave64(uint64_t a, uint64_t b) {
    uint64_t a_spread = part1by1(a);
    uint64_t b_spread = part1by1(b);

    struct uint128_t result;
    result.lo = (a_spread << 1) | b_spread;
    result.hi = (a_spread >> 63) | (b_spread >> 63);
    return result;
}
```

This implementation uses the classic part1by1 technique to spread bits, then combines them with simple shifts and OR operations, achieving the required bit alternation without loops or conditionals.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of fixed-depth carry logic and 128-bit arithmetic without loops—critical in high-throughput code paths.

## Key Concepts

- **Carry-lookahead across 4×32-bit lanes**: Break 128 bits into four 32-bit chunks for parallel carry computation.
- **Propagate (P) and Generate (G) signals**: P = a ^ b (carry passes through), G = a & b (carry generated).
- **Fixed-depth carry computation**: Uses only OR/AND operations, achieving O(1) depth.
- **Correct 128-bit carry propagation**: Ensure carries propagate correctly across all four lanes.

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 add128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
    uint64_t a0 = (uint32_t)a.lo;
    uint64_t a1 = (uint32_t)(a.lo >> 32);
    uint64_t a2 = (uint32_t)a.hi;
    uint64_t a3 = (uint32_t)(a.hi >> 32);

    uint64_t b0 = (uint32_t)b.lo;
    uint64_t b1 = (uint32_t)(b.lo >> 32);
    uint64_t b2 = (uint32_t)b.hi;
    uint64_t b3 = (uint32_t)(b.hi >> 32);

    uint64_t p0 = a0 ^ b0, g0 = a0 & b0;
    uint64_t p1 = a1 ^ b1, g1 = a1 & b1;
    uint64_t p2 = a2 ^ b2, g2 = a2 & b2;
    uint64_t p3 = a3 ^ b3, g3 = a3 & b3;

    uint64_t c1 = g0;
    uint64_t c2 = g0 | (p0 & g1);
    uint64_t c3 = g0 | (p0 & g1) | (p0 & p1 & g2);
    uint64_t c4 = g0 | (p0 & g1) | (p0 & p1 & g2) | (p0 & p1 & p2 & g3);

    uint128 r;
    r.lo = (p3 ^ c4) | ((p2 ^ c3) << 32) | ((p1 ^ c2) << 64) | ((p0 ^ c1) << 96);
    r.hi = (p3 ^ c4) >> 32;
    return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive bit-run counts without loops, handling 128-bit arithmetic and portable popcounts.

## Key Concepts

- Detect run starts with mask m = x & ~(x << 1).
- Implement 128-bit left shift across hi/lo: (hi,lo) << 1 becomes (hi<<1 | lo>>63, lo<<1).
- Compute popcount on a 128-bit value by summing 64-bit SWAR popcounts on hi and lo.

## Code Example

